# 6. Submission handling

When user submits, do not directly call Groq.

Do this:

```txt
User submits wish
↓
Backend saves wish as pending
↓
User gets success response
↓
Worker later moderates 10–15 wishes together
```

This is better because:

* user does not wait for AI
* fewer Groq calls
* less cost
* less chance of timeout
* backend stays stable during traffic spikes

---

# 7. Local abusive filter first

Before AI, run cheap local checks.

Reject immediately if message contains:

* URLs
* phone numbers
* emails
* HTML/script
* repeated spam text
* obvious abusive words
* political/religious hate
* sexual abuse
* teacher insults

Example validation rules:

```ts
function validateWishMessage(message: string) {
  const cleaned = message.trim();

  if (cleaned.length < 5) {
    return "Wish is too short.";
  }

  if (cleaned.length > 250) {
    return "Wish is too long.";
  }

  if (/https?:\/\//i.test(cleaned) || /www\./i.test(cleaned)) {
    return "Links are not allowed.";
  }

  if (/<[^>]*>/g.test(cleaned)) {
    return "HTML is not allowed.";
  }

  if (/\b\d{10}\b/.test(cleaned)) {
    return "Phone numbers are not allowed.";
  }

  if (/(.)\1{8,}/.test(cleaned)) {
    return "Spam text is not allowed.";
  }

  return null;
}
```

Then abusive words:

```ts
const abusiveWords = [
  // English abusive words
  "fuck",
  "bitch",
  "bastard",

  // Roman Hindi / Hinglish abuses
  "madarchod",
  "bhenchod",
  "behenchod",
  "chutiya",
  "gandu",
  "randi",

  // Hindi script abuses can also be added here
];
```

Do normalized matching:

```ts
function normalizeText(text: string) {
  return text
    .toLowerCase()
    .normalize("NFKD")
    .replace(/[^\p{L}\p{N}\s]/gu, "")
    .replace(/\s+/g, " ")
    .trim();
}
```

If local filter catches clear abuse:

```ts
status = "rejected";
moderation.method = "local";
```

No AI needed.

